 over two and a half million lenses  that are used you know by 250 million  people every day on snapchat so what  could i do with it exactly i mean just  give me an example oh there's a wide  range of things you could try on a new  pair of shoes you could solve a math  problem by pointing your camera at it uh  sometimes you can even just overlay fun  effects on your face if you want to you  know express yourself with your friends  and when you were starting snapchat the  thing i like you don't do likes up or  down you know that's that's very  debilitating for a lot of people that  what was your what was your intention  when you started the company years ago  what were you trying to do well bobby  and i grew up with social media and what  we found was that people were only  sharing like that one percent of their  life that's really pretty really perfect  so there was a lot of pressure to get  attention you know this kind of big game  to get social attention to get comments  and likes online and so we wanted a  different way to express ourselves with  our friends and so when we built  snapchat there weren't public likes and  comments and actually the content in  your story for example disappears after  24 hours and that makes people feel more  comfortable expressing themselves  because every day is a new day well gail  mentioned the word debilitating and snap  is one of few social media companies  that are being sued by parents who have  lost their children due to suicide what  do you say to those who look at social  media might look at snap and say  this is unhealthy for teens you know  because they get lost in this vortex of  looking at things that might not be a  reflection of who they are  yeah well we purposely built snapchat to  support real friendships so people have  to both opt in to a conversation there  as we mentioned no public likes and  comments so there isn't that social  pressure to look pretty imperfect all  the time if you look at our content  platform we actually curate that content  platform so we decide what's allowed to  be distributed on snapchat and we work  with publishing partners and share  revenue with them to make sure that our  community has accurate and timely  information we also make available a lot  of mental health resources and the  reason why we're so focused on real  friendship is because there's a clear  relationship between friendship and  mental health in fact one of the best  predictors the number one predictor of  mental health and your ability to  withstand a depressive event is a real  friendship and is your ability to just  share how you're feeling and it doesn't  have to be a deep conversation just a  friend checking in and saying hey how  are you can make a huge difference so  we've actually built tools as well to  encourage friends to check in on each  other and see how they're doing because  that real friendship is so important to  mental health it's interesting we've  talked a regulation for a second you and  mark zuckerberg clearly have a  disagreement about whether uh virtual  reality in the metaverse is the future  or augmented reality uh zuckerberg has  been calling for some regulation of  social media do you agree with him that  things need to be done at the  congressional level and what would you  support  we definitely support thoughtful  regulation but i think the really  challenging thing in the technology  industry is that technology moves so  quickly that regulation is not a  substitute for moral responsibility i  think businesses need to take  responsibility for the products that  they offer especially when they reach so  many mil you know so many people we  reach over 600 million people and so we  need to be really thoughtful and  deliberate building our products and  making sure that they have a positive  impact in the world so i think  thoughtful regulation is important and  there's certainly a lot of progress  there europe's been moving uh you know  very quickly 